"Sound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.10149v1)\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.10149v1\",\"lineno\":338}); Total lines: 793\nL307: Minimal premises. The replay above held every other-variable fact at $\\sigma^{\\star}$, but most were not actually consulted. Recording the maximal set would inflate the graph and, worse, manufacture spurious dependencies that the debloater would later have to mask around.\nL308: MinPremises (Alg. 【17†4.2】) trims them via a drop-to-top rule: candidates are individual facts $(v^{\\prime},k^{\\prime})$ about variables $v^{\\prime}$ that $c$ references, and the procedure tentatively drops each in turn (widening the affected variable toward $\\top$ via $\\mathit{state}(\\cdot)$ from Alg. 【17†4.2】), replays $\\mathrm{transfer}_{c}$, and discards a candidate if the target still emerges. Soundness follows from §【16†4.1】’s monotonicity: a fact whose absence the target survives was not consulted.\nL309: Visit order is arbitrary; different orders yield different irreducible sets, but proof search needs some proof, not a minimum-cardinality one.\nL310: Algorithm 2. MinPremises via drop-to-top.\nL311: \nL312: \nL313: \nL314: Notation. For a fact set $S\\subseteq F$, let $\\mathit{state}(S)$ denote the abstract state assigning $v\\mapsto\\top$ and each other variable $u$ the narrowest abstract value consistent with the facts $\\{(u,k^{\\prime})\\in S\\}$.\nL315: \nL316: Input: Constraint $c$, target fact $(v,k)$, fixpoint $\\sigma^{\\star}$\nL317: \nL318: Output: Irreducible $P\\subseteq F$ such that $\\mathrm{transfer}_{c}(\\mathit{state}(P))(v)\\Vdash(v,k)$\nL319: 1:$P\\leftarrow\\{(v^{\\prime},k^{\\prime}):v^{\\prime}\\in\\mathrm{vars}(c)\\setminus\\{v\\},\\ k^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{K},\\ \\sigma^{\\star}(v^{\\prime})\\Vdash(v^{\\prime},k^{\\prime})\\}$\nL320: \nL321: 2:for each $p\\in P$ (arbitrary order) do\nL322: \nL323: 3: $\\sigma^{\\prime}\\leftarrow\\mathit{state}(P\\setminus\\{p\\})$ $\\triangleright$ widen by dropping $p$\nL324: \nL325: 4: if $\\mathrm{transfer}_{c}(\\sigma^{\\prime})(v)\\Vdash(v,k)$ then\nL326: \nL327: 5:  $P\\leftarrow P\\setminus\\{p\\}$ $\\triangleright$ $p$ unused\nL328: \nL329: 6:return $P$\nL330: Algorithm for minimizing the premises of a derived fact. Starting from all relevant fixpoint facts, it drops each premise in turn, reconstructs the abstract state, and permanently removes the premise when replaying the constraint still derives the target fact.\nL331: \nL332: Complexity. Write $n=|\\mathcal{C}|$ and $d$ for the maximum number of variables referenced by any single constraint.\nL333: ###### Theorem 4.2 (Construction complexity).\nL334: \nL335: Alg. 【17†4.2】 runs in time $O(n\\cdot d^{3})$ and produces a graph with $O(|\\mathcal{V}|)$ fact nodes, $O(n\\cdot d)$ constraint nodes, and $O(n\\cdot d^{2})$ edges.\nL336: \nL337: Usually, a constraint has a limited number of variables, so $d$ is a small constant, thus the construction is near-linear in $n$. The proof sketch appears in Appendix 【33†D】.\nL338: ### 4.3. Provenance-Guided Debloating\nL339: \nL340: Debloating selects constraints whose removal preserves the witness set. An entailment filter proposes candidates; a removal loop removes them one at a time, using AND/OR proof search on $G$ to certify each removal. We describe these components in order, then argue soundness.\nL341: Entailment filter. A constraint $c$ is a candidate, written $c\\in\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathsf{f}}$, when the fixpoint $\\sigma^{\\star}$ already implies its enforced property: $\\sigma^{\\star}\\Vdash_{\\mathsf{ent}}c$. We define $\\Vdash_{\\mathsf{ent}}$ by cases on the constraint shape; two are representative, and the remainder follow the same template.\nL342: \nL343:   * •\nL344: Lookup $c:\\ v\\in T$ where $T$ is a single-column range table containing exactly the integers in $[t_{\\ell},t_{u}]$ — holds when $\\sigma^{\\star}(v)\\Vdash v\\in[t_{\\ell},t_{u}]$.\nL345: \nL346:   * •\nL347: \nL348: Gate $c:\\ e(\\vec{v})=0$ — holds when $\\sigma^{\\star}(v_{i})=\\{a_{i}\\}$ for all $i$ and $e(\\vec{a})=0$.\nL349: Here $\\sigma^{\\star}(v)\\Vdash v\\in[t_{\\ell},t_{u}]$ abbreviates the joint $\\mathit{lo}/\\mathit{hi}$ atom check, and $\\sigma^{\\star}(v_{i})=\\{a_{i}\\}$ denotes a singleton interval (lo and hi coincide at $a_{i}$). For lookup tables that are not of this form, the range rule does not apply. Analogous cases handle bit decompositions, R1CS products, and equals-zero constraints. The filter is sound but incomplete: a redundant constraint whose shape does not match any implemented case is simply retained.\nL350: Provability under a mask. A subset $M\\subseteq N$ is a mask, modeling constraint nodes that the debloater has tentatively disabled; masks arise because removals are applied incrementally and provability must be re-checked after each. Provability under $M$ has AND/OR semantics: a fact $f\\in F$ is provable iff some unmasked constraint node $\\nu\\in N\\setminus M$ targeting $f$ is itself provable (OR), and a constraint node $\\nu=(c,f,P)\\in N\\setminus M$ is provable iff every premise $p\\in P$ is provable (AND).\nL351: We take the least fixed point of these clauses: a fact is provable only when a finite, acyclic derivation rooted at axiom nodes witnesses it; cyclic derivations do not establish provability.\nL352: Proof search. The removal loop needs to decide, for a fact $f$ and a mask $M$, whether $f$ is provable in $G$ under $M$. We compute this by AND/OR depth-first search (Alg. 【18†4.3】). $\\textsc{ProveOr}(f,M)$ succeeds when some unmasked constraint node $\\nu$ targeting $f$ succeeds; a constraint node $\\nu\\in N\\setminus M$ succeeds when every premise of $\\nu$ does.\nL353: The $\\mathit{visiting}$ set returns false on revisits to a node still on the search stack, refusing to derive a fact from a circular proof and thereby ruling out the premise-asymmetry cycles of Challenge B (§【14†3.3】). The $\\mathit{proven}$ and $\\mathit{disproven}$ memos record each fact node’s result on return, so subsequent references within the same query short-circuit. Each node and edge is thus traversed at most once per query, giving $O(|F|+|N|+|E|)$ time.\nL354: Algorithm 3. AND/OR proof search under a mask.\nL355: \nL356: \nL357: \nL358: Input: Graph $G=(F\\cup N,E)$, target $f\\in F$, mask $M\\subseteq N$\nL359: \nL360: Output: true iff $f$ is provable in $G$ under $M$\nL361: \nL362: Driver:\nL363: \nL364: 1:$\\mathit{visiting},\\,\\mathit{proven},\\,\\mathit{disproven}\\leftarrow\\emptyset$\nL365: \nL366: 2:return $\\textsc{ProveOr}(f,M)$\nL367: \nL368: function $\\textsc{ProveOr}(f,M)$:\nL369: \nL370: 3: if $f\\in\\mathit{proven}$ return true $\\triangleright$ memo\nL371: \nL372: 4: if $f\\in\\mathit{disproven}$ return false $\\triangleright$ memo\nL373: 5: if $f\\in\\mathit{visiting}$ return false $\\triangleright$ cycle\nL374: \nL375: 6: $\\mathit{visiting}\\leftarrow\\mathit{visiting}\\cup\\{f\\}$; $r\\leftarrow\\textsc{false}$\nL376: \nL377: 7: for each $\\nu$ with $(\\nu\\to f)\\in E$ do\nL378: \nL379: 8:  if $\\textsc{ProveAnd}(\\nu,M)$ then $r\\leftarrow\\textsc{true}$; break\nL380: \nL381: 9: $\\mathit{visiting}\\leftarrow\\mathit{visiting}\\setminus\\{f\\}$\nL382: \nL383: 10: if $r$ then $\\mathit{proven}\\leftarrow\\mathit{proven}\\cup\\{f\\}$ else $\\mathit{disproven}\\leftarrow\\mathit{disproven}\\cup\\{f\\}$\nL384: \nL385: 11: return $r$\nL386: function $\\textsc{ProveAnd}(\\nu=(c,f^{\\prime},P),M)$:\nL387: \nL388: 12: if $\\nu\\in M$ return false\nL389: \nL390: 13: if $\\nu\\in\\mathit{visiting}$ return false $\\triangleright$ cycle\nL391: \nL392: 14: $\\mathit{visiting}\\leftarrow\\mathit{visiting}\\cup\\{\\nu\\}$; $r\\leftarrow\\textsc{true}$\nL393: \nL394: 15: for each $p\\in P$ do\nL395: \nL396: 16:  if $\\neg\\textsc{ProveOr}(p,M)$ then $r\\leftarrow\\textsc{false}$; break\nL397: \nL398: 17: $\\mathit{visiting}\\leftarrow\\mathit{visiting}\\setminus\\{\\nu\\}$; return $r$\nL399: Cycle-aware AND-OR proof-search algorithm under a mask. The driver initializes visiting, proven, and disproven sets. The OR procedure searches for an unmasked supporting constraint, while the AND procedure recursively proves every premise; stack revisits fail to prevent circular proofs.\nL400: Removal loop. For each candidate $c\\in\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathsf{f}}$, its proof obligations $\\mathrm{Obl}(c)\\subseteq F$ are the fact nodes the filter consulted when admitting $c$.\nL401: The two filter rules above instantiate $\\mathrm{Obl}(c)$ as follows: a lookup entailed by $\\sigma^{\\star}(v)\\Vdash v\\in[t_{\\ell},t_{u}]$ contributes the $\\mathit{lo}$ and $\\mathit{hi}$ nodes of $v$; a gate entailed by $\\sigma^{\\star}(v_{i})=\\{a_{i}\\}$ for all $i$ contributes the $\\mathit{lo}$ and $\\mathit{hi}$ nodes of every $v_{i}$ (which together pin $v_{i}$ to $a_{i}$).\nL402: If every fact in $\\mathrm{Obl}(c)$ remains provable under the cumulative mask of prior removals, the loop removes $c$ and adds its constraint nodes to the mask (Alg. 【18†4.3】).\nL403: Algorithm 4. Removal loop for provenance-grounded debloating.\nL404: \nL405: \nL406: \nL407: Input: Candidates $\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathsf{f}}$, provenance graph $G=(F\\cup N,E)$, ordering $\\pi$ on $\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathsf{f}}$\nL408: \nL409: Output: Removal set $\\mathcal{R}$\nL410: \nL411: 1:$\\mathcal{R}\\leftarrow\\emptyset$; $M\\leftarrow\\emptyset$\nL412: \nL413: 2:for each $c\\in\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathsf{f}}$ in order $\\pi$ do\nL414: \nL415: 3: $M_{c}\\leftarrow\\{\\,\\nu=(c^{\\prime},\\cdot,\\cdot)\\in N:c^{\\prime}=c\\,\\}$ $\\triangleright$ $c$’s constraint nodes\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.10149v1)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.10149v1\",\"lineno\":558}); Total lines: 793\nL551: Table 【97†3】 reports the constraint reduction achieved by each tool on the five RQ3 architectures. $|C|$ is the R1CS constraint count (baseline). “O2” is Circom O2; “Distilling” is Albert et al.’s approach; “CirC” is CirC’s IR optimizations; “Clap” and “korrekt” run on the zkml compilation of the same architectures; and “No Graph” is our debloater with the provenance graph disabled, falling back to per-candidate re-verification (§【21†6.1】). The final column is our full debloater.\nL552: Circom O2 and Distilling. Circom O2 and Distilling achieve no reduction on four of the five circuits. The sole exception is PTB-RNN, where both remove $0.2$% of constraints, which is a negligible improvement. This is expected: both tools target algebraic simplifications (constant folding, linear-combination merging) that do not reason about cross-constraint entailment.\nL553: CirC. We render each circuit in ZoKrates and compile through CirC with and without IR optimizations, including constant folding, variable scalarization, and oblivious array elimination. CirC achieves at most $0.4$% reduction: these ML circuits lack the program features (oblivious arrays, hashing, RAM) targeted by its passes.\nL554: Clap and Halo2-Analyzer (korrekt). Both tools remove nothing on any circuit (Table 【97†3】). The zeros are structural, not incidental. Korrekt flags gates that are statically dead: polynomials that abstractly evaluate to zero on every active row via zero constants, zero scale factors, or known-zero fixed cells. We verified that every gate expression in these circuits abstract-evaluates to non-constant, because zkml activates each gate template exactly on the rows it uses and embeds only non-zero weights.\nL555: Clap’s range-check elimination drops a check only when a syntactically identical check on the same signal appears elsewhere; after equality merging, every lookup in these circuits enforces a distinct expression, leaving nothing to drop. Both tools ask whether a check is ever active or duplicated; neither asks whether an active, unique check is entailed, which is where the redundancy resides (22.3–48.7% on the same circuits, Table 【95†2】).\nL556: Exact SMT (QF_NIA). At the scale of Table 【97†3】, the exact method is out of reach. We therefore run it on scaled-down instances of the five RQ3 architectures (8–680 neurons; 116–7.2K constraints). Within the 24-hour budget, only the smallest instance, MLP (116 constraints), terminates, and it takes 11.4 hours to do so. Its removal set exactly matches the one our tool computes in 0.06 s. The other four exceed the budget.\nL557: Exact entailment is thus the right specification but not a scalable algorithm: our provenance-guided approximation reaches the same answer five orders of magnitude faster.\nL558: No-Graph ablation. On LSTM, No-Graph reaches the same $51.6$% reduction (in $42$ min) as our full debloater; on the other four circuits, it exhausts the 4-hour budget, each candidate triggering a fixpoint recomputation over the entire constraint set.\nL559: LSTM is the outlier for a structural reason: its number of constraints entering analysis (total minus equality constraints) is the smallest in the corpus ($966$ versus $1{,}140$–$13{,}014$ for the others), so each per-candidate fixpoint is correspondingly cheap. Our provenance graph replaces every fixpoint with an on-graph proof search over the candidate’s premises, finishing all five circuits in $4$ s (LSTM) to $11$ min (LeNet).\nL560: At larger scale in RQ1 (tens of millions of constraints), the same $O(n^{2})$ cost may take days; the graph is what makes that scale tractable.\nL561: Our tool. Our tool reduces constraints by $15.90$% (RNN) to $51.60$% (LSTM), the only substantial reductions within budget across all five circuits. GRU and LSTM compose multiple checked gates over shared hidden-state signals per timestep, creating denser entailment opportunities than simpler recurrent and feed-forward circuits.\nL562: Answer to RQ3. Syntactic tools achieve at most $0.40$% reduction (Circom O2, Distilling, CirC) or none at all (Clap, korrekt); exact SMT terminates only on a 116-constraint instance, confirming our result after 11.4 hours. Our tool alone removes redundant checks at substantial rates, achieving $15.90$–$51.60$% reduction.\nL563: ## 7. Discussion\nL564: Incompleteness. Exact maximum debloating is intractable at our target scale (Challenge C, §【14†3.3】). Our sound but incomplete analysis uses interval, known-bits, and constant domains, template-based entailment rules, and greedy removal. These polynomial-time approximations may miss redundant checks, but every removal remains justified by provenance-guided proof search over the surviving constraints.\nL565: Missed removals affect optimization, not soundness; our evaluation demonstrates substantial reductions on circuits with tens of millions of constraints.\nL566: Scope. Our tool targets cross-constraint entailment among soundness checks, which is the dominant redundancy class in ML inference circuits. Algebraic simplifications (constant folding, linear-combination merging, common subexpression elimination) lie outside this scope; tools in §【10†2.3】 already cover them. The two classes touch disjoint constraints, so the passes can compose.\nL567: Generality on Non-ML Domains. In principle, our method is general: it consumes only R1CS and PLONKish constraint systems over $\\mathbb{F}_{p}$, the generic formats that ZK frameworks emit regardless of the source domain. Only the density of redundancy is ML-specific, since gadget-templated compilation replicates the range, sign, and decomposition checks that drive our gains.\nL568: The same checks recur beyond ML, although our evidence covers only ML circuits: signature-verification (【98†0xPARC, 2022】) and bignum arithmetic circuits (【99†Kosba et al., 2018】) range-check limb decompositions in the same way, so they are natural next targets. The achievable reduction there is likely smaller, since field arithmetic and Merkle-path hashing, rather than such checks, account for most of their constraints.\nL569: Porting stays within the framework: a new domain needs abstract domains and transfer functions matched to its invariants, which we leave to future work.\nL570: Scalability on Complex ML Models. Modern ZK ML workloads increasingly target transformers and large language models. These compile from the same operators as our benchmarks and therefore emit the same checks, so we expect the method to extend to them. Consistent with this, BERT-tiny yields $24.90$–$37.40$% reduction (§【22†6.2】), stable as the circuit scales $32\\times$ (§【23†6.3】). We presume the trend continues beyond this scale, though end-to-end evaluation is currently precluded by prover memory.\nL571: It is a bottleneck for ZK ML at large rather than for our analysis, with GPT-2 alone requiring roughly 1 TB of RAM (【50†Chen et al., 2024】).\nL572: ## 8. Related Work\nL573: ZK machine learning and acceleration. A broad literature authors circuits for specific model families with custom gadgets and protocol choices: zkCNN (【100†Liu et al., 2021】), vCNN (【101†Lee et al., 2024】), and pvCNN (【102†Weng et al., 2023】) for CNNs; zkLLM (【103†Sun et al., 2024】) for language models; and works on decision-tree predictions (【104†Zhang et al., 2020】), proofs of training (【105†Garg et al., 2023】), and R1CS gadgets for NN primitives (【106†Feng et al., 2021】).\nL574: A complementary line reduces proving cost without post-processing the constraints: folding schemes (【107†Kothapalli et al., 2022】; 【108†Kothapalli and Setty, 2024】) amortize repeated structure; lookup arguments (【109†Gabizon and Williamson, 2020】; 【110†Haböck, 2022】; 【111†Setty et al., 2024】; 【112†Arun et al., 2024】) offload nonlinearities to tables; GKR-based protocols (【113†Xie et al., 2019】; 【114†Wahby et al., 2018】) prove layered computations via sumcheck; and hardware work (【115†Ma et al., 2023】; 【116†Li et al., 2025】) lowers prover cost directly.\nL575: These choices are made at authoring time or per-operation; our debloater is a post-pass that removes constraints already redundant by composition, and is therefore orthogonal.\nL576: DNN verification.\nL577: Neural-network verifiers use abstract interpretation and bound propagation to prove input-output properties: AI^{2} (【117†Gehr et al., 2018】) introduced abstract interpretation for certification; Reluplex (【71†Katz et al., 2017】) and Marabou (【118†Katz et al., 2019】) combine Simplex with ReLU case splitting; ERAN (【73†Singh et al., 2019】; 【119†Singh et al., 2018】) uses zonotope and polyhedral abstractions; and $\\alpha,\\beta$-CROWN (【72†Zhang et al., 2018】; 【120†Xu et al., 2021】; 【121†Wang et al., 2021】) combines linear bounds with branch-and-bound; and DeepSRGR (【122†Yang et al., 2021】) tightens neuron bounds via LP.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.10149v1)\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.10149v1\",\"lineno\":235}); Total lines: 793\nL213: The optimisation compounds the hardness: it ranges over $2^{|\\mathcal{C}|}$ candidates, and Challenge B shows subset validity does not decompose into singleton checks, placing the exact problem in $\\Sigma_{2}^{P}$.\nL214: We therefore forgo exact methods for a polynomial-time, sound-by-construction over-approximation. Three practical limits shape its design. One at a time: with $2^{|\\mathcal{C}|}$ subsets to enumerate, we grow $\\mathcal{R}$ greedily, accepting each addition only if safely removable given prior commits; the cumulative mask is also the snapshot Challenge B’s proof search consults, so one-at-a-time is what makes cycle-aware checking possible.\nL215: Shared work: rerunning the analysis after each tentative removal would multiply its cost by $|\\mathcal{C}|$, not scalable (§【20†6】); we instead compute the derivations once and reuse them via the provenance graph. Targeted obligations: since checking even single-constraint redundancy is hard, a heuristic entailment filter (§【18†4.3】) flags constraints whose enforced property the fixpoint already entails and names the facts the proof search must re-derive.\nL216: §【15†4】 instantiates these three responses as a single pipeline.\nL217: ## 4. Approach\nL218: \nL219: For the check classes handled by our entailment rules, removing a constraint $c$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ is safe iff the facts $c$ enforces are still derivable from $\\mathcal{C}\\setminus\\{c\\}$; our pipeline turns that derivability test into a tractable computation in three phases:\nL220:  | $$\\mathcal{C}\\;\\xrightarrow[\\,\\lx@sectionsign\\ref{sec:abstract-interp}\\,]{\\text{\\small abstract\nL221: interp.}}\\;\\sigma^{\\star}\\;\\xrightarrow[\\,\\lx@sectionsign\\ref{sec:provenance}\\,]{\\text{\\small provenance}}\\;G\\;\\xrightarrow[\\,\\lx@sectionsign\\ref{sec:debloating}\\,]{\\text{\\small debloating}}\\;\\mathcal{R}.$$  |\nL222: Abstract interpretation proves which facts hold of each variable, computing a fixpoint $\\sigma^{\\star}$ over three abstract domains (intervals, known bits, constants). The provenance graph $G$ records, for each fact in $\\sigma^{\\star}$, which constraints proved it from which premise facts. Provenance-guided debloating then verifies, for each candidate $c$, that the facts $c$ enforces remain derivable in $G$ after $c$ is masked out; if so, $c$ is removed, yielding the removal set $\\mathcal{R}$.\nL223: In Appendix 【34†E】 we give an end-to-end example of the three phases on a five-constraint circuit distilled from §【13†3.2】.\nL224: ### 4.1. Abstract Interpretation over Constraints\nL225: \nL226: Abstract domain. Each variable $v\\in\\mathcal{V}$ is tracked by an abstract value drawn from a reduced product of three lattice domains:\nL227: \nL228:   1. (1)\nL229: \nL230: Constant: either a known field element or $\\top$ (unknown).\nL231: \nL232:   2. (2)\nL233: \nL234: Interval: a range $[\\ell,u]\\subseteq\\mathbb{Z}$ bounding all possible values of $v$ when interpreted as a signed integer.\nL235: \nL236:   3. (3)\nL237: Known bits: a bitmask $m$ and a value pattern $b$, recording that for every bit position $i$ where $m_{i}=1$, the $i$-th bit of $v$ equals $b_{i}$.\nL238: \nL239: The three components cross-propagate: a refinement in one domain sharpens the others via standard reduced-product rules (【54†Cousot and Cousot, 1977】; 【76†Cousot and Cousot, 1979】), as in LLVM’s KnownBits analysis (【77†The LLVM Project, 0000】).\nL240: Transfer functions. Each constraint type defines a transfer function that refines the abstract values of its variables. Transfer functions handle arithmetic gates (addition, multiplication, scaling, and their inverses), lookup tables (intersecting a variable’s abstract value with the table’s range), and bit decompositions (linking interval bounds to individual bit facts). Propagation is non-directional: $x+y=z$ may tighten any of $x$, $y$, or $z$ from the other two.\nL241: We show three representative rules in inference-rule form; the remainder follow the same template. Premises above the line are the abstract-state facts consulted; the conclusion below the line is the contributed refinement. $\\sigma$ denotes the current state; $A,B$ abbreviate $\\sigma(a),\\sigma(b)$; $\\sqsubseteq$ is the abstract-interval order (sharper bounds are smaller); and $A\\otimes B$ is the smallest interval containing $\\{ab:a\\in A,\\ b\\in B\\}$ (interval multiplication).\nL242:  | $$\\begin{array}[]{c}\\textsc{Lookup}\\\\[2.0pt]\nL243: \\dfrac{v\\in T\\hskip 9.24994pt\\mathrm{range}(T)=[t_{\\ell},t_{u}]}{[t_{\\ell},t_{u}]\\sqsubseteq\\sigma(v)}\\end{array}\\hskip 18.49988pt\\begin{array}[]{c}\\textsc{Bitify}\\\\[2.0pt]\nL244: \\dfrac{\\begin{array}[]{c}v=\\textstyle\\sum_{i<n}b_{i}\\cdot 2^{i}\\hskip 9.24994ptb_{i}\\in\\{0,1\\}\\\\[2.0pt]\nL245: \\sigma(b_{i})=\\{0\\}\\text{ for }i\\geq k\\end{array}}{[0,\\,2^{k}-1]\\sqsubseteq\\sigma(v)}\\end{array}$$  |\nL246:  | $$\\textsc{Quadratic gate}\\hskip 9.24994pt\\dfrac{a\\cdot b+c=0\\hskip 9.24994pt\\sigma(a)=A\\hskip 9.24994pt\\sigma(b)=B}{-(A\\otimes B)\\sqsubseteq\\sigma(c)}$$  |\nL247: Symmetric variants are elided: Bitify also propagates backward, and Quadratic gate solves backward for $a$ or $b$: rearranging $a\\cdot b+c=0$ divides by the other operand, giving $-(\\sigma(c)/\\sigma(b))\\sqsubseteq\\sigma(a)$ and symmetrically for $b$. The backward rule fires only when the divisor’s range is sign-determined (in particular, excludes zero), which makes the interval division well defined; otherwise it contributes nothing.\nL248: Soundness under field wrap-around. Witnesses live in $\\mathbb{F}_{p}$, not $\\mathbb{Z}$. We interpret a field element as its canonical signed representative in the window $W=[-(p-1)/2,\\,(p-1)/2]$.\nL249: A transfer rule is allowed to contribute an integer fact only when the concrete operation it abstracts is unique in this signed interpretation: for addition and scaling, the operand ranges and the resulting range must stay inside $W$; for multiplication, the full interval product must stay inside $W$; and for backward rules such as solving a quadratic gate for one operand, the divisor/range side conditions used by the rule must also keep the inferred range inside $W$.\nL250: Lookup and bit-decomposition rules are treated as integer facts only for table entries or bit widths whose represented values lie inside the same signed window. We enforce this discipline by widening any out-of-window conclusion to $\\top$:\nL251:  | $$\\textsc{WrapGuard}\\quad\\dfrac{[\\ell,u]\\sqsubseteq\\sigma(v)\\;\\text{(derived above)}\\quad[\\ell,u]\\not\\subseteq W}{\\top\\sqsubseteq\\sigma(v)}$$  |\nL252: \nL253: Thus, any modular wrap-around that would make the integer reading ambiguous causes the analysis to keep no refinement from that rule. With the wrap-around guard, the analysis performs a refinement only when integer and modular arithmetic agree on it, so every kept fact holds for every witness (wrap-around or not).\nL254: Fixpoint computation. Variables start at $\\top$, except those pinned to circuit constants (e.g., fixed-column entries or hard-coded values), which start at the corresponding constant. A standard worklist re-applies each constraint’s transfer function whenever one of its variables is refined.\nL255: Soundness, termination, and complexity. Each transfer function is locally sound: for every constraint $c$ and every witness $w\\models c$, applying $\\mathrm{transfer}_{c}$ to any abstract state that over-approximates $w$ yields a state that still over-approximates $w$. We verify this condition per constraint type and take it as a precondition on the rules.\nL256: Local soundness, monotonicity ($\\sigma_{1}\\sqsubseteq\\sigma_{2}\\Rightarrow\\mathrm{transfer}_{c}(\\sigma_{1})\\sqsubseteq\\mathrm{transfer}_{c}(\\sigma_{2})$), and the bounded height of the lattice (every interval lies in $W$ by WrapGuard) are the standard ingredients of the worklist fixpoint construction (【54†Cousot and Cousot, 1977】; 【76†Cousot and Cousot, 1979】): chaotic iteration (a worklist with arbitrary dequeue order) reaches a fixpoint $\\sigma^{\\star}$ that over-approximates every $w\\models\\mathcal{C}$.\nL257: With $n=|\\mathcal{C}|$ constraints, $d$ the maximum constraint arity, and $h$ the height of the interval lattice over $W$, the construction runs in $O(n\\cdot d\\cdot h)$ time.\nL258: ### 4.2. Provenance Graph\nL259: The fixpoint $\\sigma^{\\star}$ proves facts but discards how they were derived; the premise asymmetry of Challenge B shows why that history matters. To handle it without re-running the fixpoint after every candidate removal, we record the derivation structure as a provenance graph (【78†Doyle, 1979】; 【79†de Kleer, 1986】): a bipartite AND/OR graph whose fact nodes (OR) hold proven facts and whose constraint nodes (AND) record which constraints, with which premise facts, established them.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/SPIRE-GMU/sum_cats/blob/main/LICENSE\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/SPIRE-GMU/sum_cats/blob/main/LICENSE: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - xuezhantong/zk-debloater · GitHub (https://github.com/xuezhantong/zk-debloater)\nciteturn6view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn4view1\",\"id\":207}); Total lines: 364\n"
